  "notes": [
   "Parts are unchanged from step 1 (13 parts); step 1 was correct.",
   "The image shows a crop of a long breadboard (columns about 30-63) but the catalogue breadboard has only 30 columns. I compressed the layout to fit: receiver legs in columns 1, 2 and 4, IR LED in 11 and 12, Nano pins in columns 16-30. All connections between parts are the same as in the image.",
   "Rows a-e of the lower half of the image are bank 't'. The Nano's top header row is in bank 'b' row i, and the wire to it is in row j. The Nano bottom header row is in row e.",
   "The Nano is plugged in with USB at the right. Its bottom header (TX1, RX0, RST, GND, D2..D12 from left) is in columns 16-30, so D9 is in column 27, D11 in column 29 and GND in column 19. Its top header (VIN, GND, RST, VUSB, A7..A0, AREF, 3V3, D13 from left) puts 3V3 in column 29. Only the pins that carry a wire or part are listed as edges.",
   "The long red wire goes from the Nano's 3V3 pin to the bottom + rail. The IR receiver is powered from that rail at 3.3 V, which is safe for the 3.3 V-only Nano.",
   "The IR receiver's three legs are VCC, GND and DAT from left to right (red wire, black wire, green wire). Its DAT leg goes to D11 by the green wire.",
   "The IR LED's anode (column 12) goes through the 330 ohm resistor and the blue wire to D9. Its cathode (column 11) goes by a black wire to the ground rail. The LED's polarity is inferred: the leg with the black wire is the cathode.",
   "The black wire from the Nano is drawn on the third pin from the left of the bottom header, which may be RST. I took it as the GND pin next to it, because it goes to the ground rail.",
   "The rail row numbers (bp.1, bp.30, bn.3, bn.11, bn.19) are arbitrary, since each rail is one strip. The bottom rail's upper row is taken as - (blue line) and its lower row as + (red line).",
   "The pin D11 for the receiver and D9 for the transmitter are read from the image; the manual text gives no pin table."
  ]
 },
